<div class="bg-surface-base min-h-screen text-neutral-200">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-8">
      <a
        href="/"
        class="text-neutral-400 hover:text-neutral-200 text-sm"
      >
        &larr; Back
      </a>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">
        Understanding Bitcoin
      </h1>
    </div>

    <p class="text-neutral-400 mb-10 text-lg leading-relaxed">
      Bitcoin is a peer-to-peer electronic cash system described in a
      <a
        href="https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf"
        class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 underline"
        target="_blank"
      >whitepaper</a>
      published in 2008 by Satoshi Nakamoto.
      This guide explains how the blockchain works and how to explore it using Bitblocks.
    </p>

    <%!-- Section: What is a Block? --%>
    <section class="mb-10" id="blocks">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-neutral-100">
        What is a Block?
      </h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        A block is a bundle of transactions confirmed by miners.
        Each block links to the previous one through a cryptographic hash, forming a chain.
      </p>

      <%!-- Visual: block chain diagram --%>
      <div class="my-5 overflow-x-auto">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-0 min-w-max font-mono text-xs">
          <%= for {label, sublabel} <- [{"Block 0", "genesis"}, {"Block 1", ""}, {"Block 2", ""}, {"Block 3", "latest →"}] do %>
            <div class="flex flex-col items-center">
              <div class="border border-neutral-600 bg-neutral-900 rounded px-3 py-2 text-center w-24">
                <div class="text-neutral-200 font-semibold"><%= label %></div>
                <div class="text-neutral-500 text-[10px] mt-0.5"><%= sublabel %></div>
                <div class="text-neutral-600 text-[10px] mt-1">txs + hash</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <%= if label != "Block 3" do %>
              <div class="text-neutral-500 px-1">←</div>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        </div>
        <p class="text-neutral-600 text-xs mt-2">
          Each block contains a hash of the previous block.
          Changing any block would break every block after it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        The very first block, called the <strong class="text-neutral-200">genesis block</strong>, was mined on January 3, 2009.
        Every block since then traces back to it.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-neutral-900 border border-neutral-800 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm">
        <p class="text-neutral-500 mb-2">Explore blocks:</p>
        <a
          href="/blocks"
          class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
        >
          Browse blocks &rarr;
        </a>
        |
        <a
          href="/block-graph?start=0&end=10"
          class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
        >
          Visualize the first 10 blocks in 3D &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%!-- Section: What is a Transaction? --%>
    <section class="mb-10" id="transactions">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-neutral-100">
        What is a Transaction?
      </h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        A transaction transfers value from one or more inputs to one or more outputs.
        Each input spends a previous output, creating a chain of ownership called the <strong class="text-neutral-200">UTXO set</strong> (Unspent Transaction Outputs).
      </p>
      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        The first transaction in every block is called the <strong class="text-neutral-200">coinbase transaction</strong>.
        It creates new coins as a reward for the miner.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-neutral-900 border border-neutral-800 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm">
        <p class="text-neutral-500 mb-2">Explore transactions:</p>
        <a
          href="/transactions"
          class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
        >
          Browse transactions &rarr;
        </a>
        |
        <a
          href="/tx-graph"
          class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
        >
          Trace a transaction's spending chain &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%!-- Section: Locking and Unlocking Scripts --%>
    <section
      class="mb-10"
      id="scripts"
    >
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-neutral-100">
        Locking and Unlocking Scripts
      </h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Every transaction output carries a <strong class="text-neutral-200">locking script</strong> (ScriptPubKey) that defines who can spend it.
        The most common form is P2PKH — Pay to Public Key Hash — which requires the spender to provide a matching public key and a valid signature.
      </p>
      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        To spend an output, a transaction input must provide an <strong class="text-neutral-200">unlocking script</strong> (ScriptSig) that satisfies the locking script.
        Together they form a small program that the network runs to verify the spend is authorized.
      </p>
      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Some outputs use <strong class="text-neutral-200">OP_RETURN</strong> — a special opcode that makes the output unspendable.
        These outputs carry data instead of value.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-neutral-900 border border-neutral-800 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm mb-3">
        <p class="text-neutral-500 mb-2">
          Example — a simple P2PKH payment (genesis block coinbase):
        </p>
        <a
          href="/transactions/4a5e1e4baab89f3a32518a88c31bc87f618f76673e2cc77ab2127b7afdeda33b"
          class="font-mono text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 break-all"
        >
          4a5e1e4baab89f3a32518a88c31bc87f618f76673e2cc77ab2127b7afdeda33b
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%!-- Section: Data on the Blockchain --%>
    <section class="mb-10" id="data">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-neutral-100">
        Data on the Blockchain
      </h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Bitcoin SV transactions can store data in <strong class="text-neutral-200">OP_RETURN</strong> outputs.
        This enables protocols like B:// (file storage), MAP (key-value data), and 1Sat Ordinals (inscriptions).
      </p>
      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Bitblocks detects and parses these protocols, making on-chain data browseable.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-neutral-900 border border-neutral-800 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm">
        <p class="text-neutral-500 mb-2">Explore protocols:</p>
        <a
          href="/protocols"
          class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
        >
          Protocol Registry &rarr;
        </a>
        |
        <a
          href="/ecosystem"
          class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
        >
          BSV Ecosystem in 3D &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%!-- Section: Identity --%>
    <section class="mb-10" id="identity">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-neutral-100">
        Identity on Bitcoin
      </h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Decentralized Identifiers (DIDs) let you create a verifiable identity without a central authority.
        Bitblocks supports DID creation, Verifiable Credentials, and vCard-format profiles.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-neutral-900 border border-neutral-800 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm">
        <p class="text-neutral-500 mb-2">Try it:</p>
        <a
          href="/did"
          class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
        >
          DID Playground &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%!-- Section: The Network --%>
    <section class="mb-10" id="network">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-neutral-100">
        The Network
      </h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Bitcoin is a network of nodes that validate and relay transactions.
        Miners compete to add the next block to the chain.
        Bitblocks connects to a BSV node via RPC to sync and display blockchain data.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-neutral-900 border border-neutral-800 rounded-lg p-4 text-sm">
        <p class="text-neutral-500 mb-2">See the network:</p>
        <a
          href="/peers"
          class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
        >
          Peer Map &rarr;
        </a>
        |
        <a
          href="/pulse"
          class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
        >
          Network Pulse &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%!-- Further Reading --%>
    <section class="mb-10" id="resources">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-neutral-100">
        Further Reading
      </h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-neutral-400 text-sm">
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf"
            class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
            target="_blank"
          >
            Bitcoin Whitepaper (PDF)
          </a>
          — The original paper by Satoshi Nakamoto.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://learnmeabitcoin.com/browser/node/"
            class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
            target="_blank"
          >
            Learn Me a Bitcoin
          </a>
          — Interactive Bitcoin learning resource.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="/about"
            class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300"
          >
            About Bitblocks
          </a>
          — Learn more about this project.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
